WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:12.873
Hello, my name is Anna Duensing, I'm a

00:00:13.144 --> 00:00:15.153
PhD candidate at Yale University, working

00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:18.123
on a dissertation about antifascism

00:00:18.124 --> 00:00:19.794
within the civil rights movement and Black

00:00:20.044 --> 00:00:21.822
radical theories of fascism in

00:00:22.189 --> 00:00:24.725
the United States from the 1930s

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:25.858
onward.

00:00:26.058 --> 00:00:28.395
And today, as part of the Fortunoff

00:00:28.562 --> 00:00:30.991
Video Archives commemoration of

00:00:30.992 --> 00:00:33.181
Black History Month, I'd like to talk

00:00:33.182 --> 00:00:35.732
to you about one aspect of that research

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:38.251
which concerns African-American military

00:00:38.252 --> 00:00:39.416
service during World War II

00:00:40.502 --> 00:00:42.031
and more generally, how soldiers and

00:00:42.032 --> 00:00:43.404
veterans have contributed to

00:00:44.192 --> 00:00:46.291
the Black freedom struggle across the 20th

00:00:46.292 --> 00:00:47.292
century.

00:00:47.762 --> 00:00:49.281
And within that work, I want to

00:00:50.012 --> 00:00:52.741
elevate the experiences of African-American

00:00:52.742 --> 00:00:55.921
GIs who confronted Nazi Germany firsthand

00:00:56.252 --> 00:00:58.561
and those who really at the heart

00:00:58.562 --> 00:01:00.391
of that experience, were eyewitness to the

00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:03.242
end stages and immediate aftermath

00:01:03.392 --> 00:01:04.532
of the Holocaust.

00:01:04.922 --> 00:01:07.231
And for the most part, these men

00:01:07.232 --> 00:01:08.584
are known as liberators today.

00:01:09.122 --> 00:01:11.033
That refers to soldiers who pushed into

00:01:11.612 --> 00:01:14.101
the crumbling Third Reich in the spring

00:01:14.102 --> 00:01:16.291
and summer of 1945 and who

00:01:16.292 --> 00:01:18.392
encountered the Buchenwald,

00:01:18.512 --> 00:01:19.942
Dora-Mittelbau, Flossenbürg,

00:01:21.002 --> 00:01:22.766
Dachau, and Mauthausen concentration

00:01:23.222 --> 00:01:24.222
camps.

00:01:24.332 --> 00:01:26.439
And the Fortunoff is home to the remarkable

00:01:26.672 --> 00:01:28.711
testimony of one such soldier.

00:01:29.192 --> 00:01:30.511
His name was Leon Bass.

00:01:31.292 --> 00:01:32.909
Bass was also a lifelong educator

00:01:33.872 --> 00:01:35.881
who spent the last four and a half decades

00:01:35.882 --> 00:01:38.191
of his life up until his death

00:01:38.192 --> 00:01:39.241
in 2015.

00:01:39.902 --> 00:01:41.701
He spent those those years speaking out

00:01:41.702 --> 00:01:43.832
about his military experience

00:01:44.132 --> 00:01:45.896
and what it meant for him to witness

00:01:46.562 --> 00:01:47.885
Buchenwald while serving in

00:01:48.782 --> 00:01:50.132
a segregated Army.

00:01:50.942 --> 00:01:52.412
Really have subsequent decades

00:01:53.222 --> 00:01:55.329
of reckoning with the Holocaust contributed

00:01:55.532 --> 00:01:57.502
to his interest in education,

00:01:57.932 --> 00:01:59.731
his involvement in the civil rights

00:01:59.732 --> 00:02:01.981
movement, and more generally, how the

00:02:01.982 --> 00:02:03.403
experience of witnessing this

00:02:04.412 --> 00:02:06.571
aspect, this culmination

00:02:06.572 --> 00:02:08.911
of Nazi persecution, shaped

00:02:08.912 --> 00:02:11.101
his understanding subsequently of

00:02:11.102 --> 00:02:12.962
U.S. race relations and

00:02:13.622 --> 00:02:15.242
more generally, the realities

00:02:16.322 --> 00:02:18.086
of ongoing racial violence and white

00:02:18.242 --> 00:02:20.081
supremacy in the United States.

00:02:20.942 --> 00:02:22.801
Now, Leon Bass was born and raised in

00:02:22.802 --> 00:02:23.802
Philadelphia.

00:02:23.912 --> 00:02:25.462
He grew up in a predominantly Black

00:02:25.682 --> 00:02:27.841
neighborhood. He went to all Black schools

00:02:28.382 --> 00:02:30.781
and he describes his parents

00:02:30.782 --> 00:02:32.011
who were sharecroppers, former

00:02:32.012 --> 00:02:33.682
sharecroppers from South Carolina.

00:02:34.322 --> 00:02:36.211
They had kept him relatively sheltered from

00:02:36.212 --> 00:02:38.521
the full force and structural

00:02:38.522 --> 00:02:40.801
violence of racism and Jim Crow

00:02:40.802 --> 00:02:42.811
segregation in the United States.

00:02:43.592 --> 00:02:45.601
However, all that changes for him when he

00:02:45.782 --> 00:02:46.861
joins the army.

00:02:47.842 --> 00:02:49.791
Twenty years old by the time the Second

00:02:49.792 --> 00:02:51.381
World War had become a reality.

00:02:51.562 --> 00:02:52.738
No, I was--I, I finished

00:02:53.872 --> 00:02:56.322
school in 1943 and I was eighteen.

00:02:57.442 --> 00:02:58.941
And that's when I became part of the

00:02:58.942 --> 00:02:59.691
military.

00:02:59.692 --> 00:03:01.652
And you-- how did you become part of the

00:03:01.804 --> 00:03:03.327
military? Did you volunteer or you were

00:03:03.328 --> 00:03:04.311
drafted?

00:03:04.312 --> 00:03:06.292
Yes. I went out and I, I volunteered

00:03:06.622 --> 00:03:08.391
and I was to go out with the next group.

00:03:09.172 --> 00:03:10.791
And when I went down to the induction

00:03:10.792 --> 00:03:13.160
center, institutional

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.309
racism smacked me right in the face

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.369
because the sergeant was

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:19.370
there and he told me

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:21.650
to go one way.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:23.799
When I went through the door and he told my

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.959
white friends to go another way because

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:29.079
my country practices promulgated

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:30.364
and promoted institutional

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:33.189
racism and the military was one of the

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:35.119
largest to do just that.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:37.260
And so I went into an all Black

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:39.972
unit, save for the officers, they were

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:41.120
white.

00:03:41.649 --> 00:03:43.989
But all of my comrades

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:45.477
in arms at the time were Black.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:49.239
Induction marked the start of a brutal

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.190
new kind of education for him,

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:54.339
beginning with two hard and disaffecting

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.649
rounds of basic training in

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:57.650
the Jim Crow South.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.775
This was first in Georgia, later in

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.869
Mississippi, followed by manoeuvers in

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:04.230
Texas, Louisiana and Arkansas.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.239
And this extended training was actually

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.219
typical for African-American GIs,

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:11.919
the majority of whom were trained in combat

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:14.619
before they were reassigned and retrained

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:16.719
in unarmed labor units.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.639
And Bass was assigned to the

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:20.949
183rd Combat Engineers.

00:04:21.819 --> 00:04:23.877
And this was a time when, as with civilian

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:25.799
life, formal and informal color

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:27.935
lines snaked across every

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:31.029
aspect of military service.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.623
White soldiers, lynch mobs, vigilante

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:35.923
groups, numerous iterations

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:38.859
of state and local, civic and law

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:40.089
enforcement agencies.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:42.009
They all policed these lines as they saw

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:43.010
fit.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.459
At the same time, Jim Crow lawmakers at the

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.799
state and federal levels vowed

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.907
to defend the social and political order of

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:52.150
Jim Crow at whatever cost.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:55.389
Even U.S. blood banks remain segregated

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:57.203
at this time, which was a hold out of

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:59.769
explicit racial science so egregious,

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:02.139
so parallel to Nazi race law that

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.509
it became one of the most heated

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.819
civil rights issues of the war years.

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.399
And in the case of the armed forces that

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.115
the stress and strain and insult of

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:13.929
these color lines became particularly

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:14.930
intensified.

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:17.799
Second class citizenship is not tolerable

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:19.569
to someone in uniform, someone who is

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:21.340
risking his life for his country.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:24.377
And many African-Americans at this time had

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.273
observed distinct connections between

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:29.559
Nazism and Jim Crow, had recognized

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.139
the parallels on the basis of racism,

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.629
white supremacy, militarism,

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:36.408
empire, and attacks on organized

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:38.240
labor.

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:40.689
So the patriotic rhetoric of the war

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:42.970
and fighting in defense of democracy,

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:44.974
the framing of World War II as a war

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:47.115
of good versus evil, freedom versus

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:50.016
tyranny, to many African-Americans, this

00:05:50.217 --> 00:05:52.285
appeared hypocritical and rather

00:05:52.419 --> 00:05:53.419
fraudulent.

00:05:54.419 --> 00:05:56.188
Was it your perception, though, that the

00:05:56.189 --> 00:05:57.749
Black troops generally, though,

00:05:58.769 --> 00:06:00.568
fully understood the fact that

00:06:01.589 --> 00:06:03.568
while the rhetoric of the war against Nazi

00:06:03.569 --> 00:06:05.698
racism and so forth was fine,

00:06:06.269 --> 00:06:07.935
in practice, the country was doing

00:06:08.039 --> 00:06:08.968
something entirely different?

00:06:08.969 --> 00:06:10.439
Yes, it was as though you were

00:06:11.069 --> 00:06:12.069
schizophrenic.

00:06:12.369 --> 00:06:14.488
Our country had-- was two personalities.

00:06:14.489 --> 00:06:16.253
You know, in one way we may make the

00:06:16.409 --> 00:06:17.578
wonderful pronouncements.

00:06:17.579 --> 00:06:18.868
You know, we we talk about our

00:06:18.869 --> 00:06:20.878
Judeo-Christian ethics and we're going to

00:06:21.029 --> 00:06:22.558
make the world a better place for democracy

00:06:22.559 --> 00:06:23.849
and all that other jazz.

00:06:24.009 --> 00:06:25.618
But then when you get down to the real

00:06:25.619 --> 00:06:26.998
thing and you start seeing the way they

00:06:26.999 --> 00:06:29.723
operate, you

00:06:29.823 --> 00:06:31.382
know, things were not, they were not in

00:06:31.383 --> 00:06:32.222
consonance.

00:06:32.223 --> 00:06:34.562
And so I began

00:06:34.563 --> 00:06:36.722
to be an angry, frustrated young

00:06:36.963 --> 00:06:37.963
Black soldier.

00:06:38.793 --> 00:06:40.202
At this time, I wanted to get out of the

00:06:40.203 --> 00:06:41.132
army.

00:06:41.133 --> 00:06:42.122
I'll be very honest with you.

00:06:42.123 --> 00:06:44.402
After my experiences, I really did not

00:06:44.403 --> 00:06:45.843
want to be in this man's army

00:06:46.593 --> 00:06:47.593
and...

00:06:49.493 --> 00:06:51.742
Especially after having to stand

00:06:51.743 --> 00:06:53.647
on a bus when

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:55.279
there were no seats in the back, having to

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:57.349
stand up for 100 miles, looking at empty

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:58.350
seats.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:01.249
That didn't endear me to,

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:02.340
to my country.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.030
I couldn't eat in a restaurant.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:06.859
I had to go around the back and knock on

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:07.860
the door to get food.

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:09.439
And I'm in a uniform.

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.732
And I saw PWs, prisoners

00:07:12.899 --> 00:07:14.968
of war from Germany being

00:07:15.089 --> 00:07:16.699
allowed to go in a restaurant to sit down

00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:18.928
to eat. And yet I was not entitled to that

00:07:18.929 --> 00:07:19.929
same opportunity.

00:07:20.879 --> 00:07:22.918
Bass's battalion shipped out from Europe in

00:07:22.919 --> 00:07:24.193
October 1944 and connected

00:07:25.439 --> 00:07:27.418
with the U.S. 3rd Army in Belgium,

00:07:27.959 --> 00:07:29.668
where they were part, they fought as part

00:07:29.669 --> 00:07:31.318
of the Battle of the Bulge, which was the

00:07:31.319 --> 00:07:33.658
last major German offensive campaign

00:07:33.869 --> 00:07:35.028
on the Western Front.

00:07:35.489 --> 00:07:37.639
And as laborers, soldiers like Bass

00:07:37.979 --> 00:07:39.448
were first on the front lines.

00:07:39.449 --> 00:07:41.128
They were building roads and bridges.

00:07:41.129 --> 00:07:43.769
They were loading equipment and supplies,

00:07:43.949 --> 00:07:45.272
disabling landmines, moving

00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:47.459
the wounded and the dead.

00:07:48.239 --> 00:07:50.488
And Bass's battalion crossed into Germany

00:07:50.489 --> 00:07:52.228
in March of 1945.

00:07:52.769 --> 00:07:55.138
And by April, they had reached the central

00:07:55.199 --> 00:07:56.878
German city of Weimar.

00:07:57.839 --> 00:07:59.113
We, we went to Weimar, set

00:08:00.209 --> 00:08:01.588
up our bivouac area, and then

00:08:03.119 --> 00:08:05.129
went immediately with an officer,

00:08:06.029 --> 00:08:07.029
about five of us.

00:08:07.259 --> 00:08:08.908
We were in the intelligence reconnaissance

00:08:08.909 --> 00:08:11.091
section of our unit and we went

00:08:11.219 --> 00:08:12.839
right to Buchenwald.

00:08:14.149 --> 00:08:16.363
And that was the day that I was

00:08:16.399 --> 00:08:18.858
to discover what had really been going

00:08:18.859 --> 00:08:21.348
on in Europe under the Nazis.

00:08:22.459 --> 00:08:23.629
Because I walked through the gates

00:08:25.759 --> 00:08:27.807
and I saw walking

00:08:28.249 --> 00:08:29.249
dead people.

00:08:30.469 --> 00:08:32.946
Seriously, walking dead people.

00:08:33.409 --> 00:08:34.709
I am not a doctor.

00:08:34.758 --> 00:08:36.768
I cannot assess things

00:08:37.638 --> 00:08:39.858
that accurately. But in just looking

00:08:39.859 --> 00:08:41.718
at these people who were skin and bone

00:08:42.709 --> 00:08:43.709
and...

00:08:44.699 --> 00:08:46.829
dressed in those pajama type uniforms,

00:08:46.969 --> 00:08:49.439
their heads clean shaved, and

00:08:50.459 --> 00:08:53.239
filled with sores due to malnutrition,

00:08:53.939 --> 00:08:55.529
and here they were coming towards us,

00:08:56.819 --> 00:08:59.188
making all kinds of guttural

00:08:59.199 --> 00:09:01.128
statements and using their own language.

00:09:01.139 --> 00:09:02.728
And it was very difficult for me to

00:09:02.729 --> 00:09:04.819
comprehend what was going on.

00:09:04.829 --> 00:09:06.419
I just looked at this in amazement and,

00:09:07.199 --> 00:09:09.582
and I said to myself,

00:09:09.719 --> 00:09:12.148
you know, my God,

00:09:12.149 --> 00:09:13.518
you know, who are these people?

00:09:15.259 --> 00:09:16.259
What have they done,

00:09:17.419 --> 00:09:18.388
what was their crime?

00:09:18.389 --> 00:09:20.627
You know, it's hard for me to try

00:09:20.659 --> 00:09:22.423
to understand why anybody could have

00:09:22.819 --> 00:09:24.262
been treated this way and I don't care what

00:09:24.263 --> 00:09:26.418
they had done, just didn't

00:09:27.199 --> 00:09:28.199
grab me.

00:09:28.219 --> 00:09:30.409
And I didn't have any way of thinking,

00:09:30.829 --> 00:09:31.829
of putting a handle on it.

00:09:32.119 --> 00:09:33.128
No frame of reference.

00:09:33.139 --> 00:09:34.139
I was only 20.

00:09:34.699 --> 00:09:36.440
When U.S. forces discovered Buchenwald,

00:09:36.441 --> 00:09:38.645
they found over 21,000

00:09:38.869 --> 00:09:40.248
people imprisoned there.

00:09:40.879 --> 00:09:42.741
And the days before their arrival, the

00:09:43.069 --> 00:09:44.931
Germans had attempted to evacuate some

00:09:45.049 --> 00:09:47.238
30,000 others, roughly

00:09:47.239 --> 00:09:49.669
a third of whom perished in the process.

00:09:50.239 --> 00:09:52.248
The earliest inmates at Buchenwald, which

00:09:52.279 --> 00:09:54.427
opened in 1937, were political

00:09:54.559 --> 00:09:57.078
prisoners. But the German SS and police

00:09:57.079 --> 00:09:59.628
had sent many Jews there, alongside Roma,

00:09:59.639 --> 00:10:01.599
Jehovah's Witnesses, military deserters,

00:10:02.359 --> 00:10:04.189
repeat criminals, and others,

00:10:04.529 --> 00:10:05.529
other groups.

00:10:05.719 --> 00:10:07.728
And at the end of the war, Buchenwald was

00:10:07.909 --> 00:10:10.128
one of roughly 44,000

00:10:10.129 --> 00:10:12.445
camps, ghettos and incarceration

00:10:12.829 --> 00:10:14.988
sites operated by Nazi Germany

00:10:15.379 --> 00:10:17.782
and its allies across Europe.

00:10:18.839 --> 00:10:20.422
Terms like Auschwitz and Bergen-Belsen,

00:10:21.199 --> 00:10:23.110
Majdanek and places like that were not,

00:10:23.209 --> 00:10:24.259
were not known to you.

00:10:24.379 --> 00:10:26.437
Even when I went into Buchenwald, I had no

00:10:26.479 --> 00:10:28.549
idea that those places existed.

00:10:29.479 --> 00:10:31.865
Those names only came to me after

00:10:32.179 --> 00:10:33.409
I got out of the service.

00:10:34.489 --> 00:10:36.679
Later, much later,

00:10:36.859 --> 00:10:38.875
I began to hear of names and places

00:10:40.429 --> 00:10:42.018
and realized that there was more than just

00:10:42.019 --> 00:10:44.144
a Buchenwald, that they were

00:10:44.239 --> 00:10:46.248
many, many, many Buchenwalds of different

00:10:46.279 --> 00:10:47.868
names all over Europe

00:10:48.889 --> 00:10:49.889
and in Russia.

00:10:50.949 --> 00:10:53.453
And that's what boggles

00:10:53.529 --> 00:10:55.195
the mind, to think that's, a group

00:10:55.809 --> 00:10:57.671
that society can organize and do it in

00:10:57.999 --> 00:11:00.268
a systematic way, such a

00:11:01.359 --> 00:11:02.359
program.

00:11:03.979 --> 00:11:06.232
To remove from

00:11:06.366 --> 00:11:08.045
the world a whole group of people

00:11:08.586 --> 00:11:10.745
and any others that didn't seem, they

00:11:10.746 --> 00:11:12.193
didn't think were worthy.

00:11:13.476 --> 00:11:15.185
Like many of his comrades,

00:11:15.966 --> 00:11:17.705
Bass initially experienced a kind of

00:11:17.706 --> 00:11:19.545
numbness about what he had seen.

00:11:20.286 --> 00:11:22.355
Military intelligence had not prepared

00:11:22.356 --> 00:11:24.545
soldiers for the realities of the camps.

00:11:24.966 --> 00:11:26.975
And within weeks, Bass was shipped off to

00:11:27.006 --> 00:11:29.615
the Philippines to finish his service.

00:11:30.276 --> 00:11:31.985
And upon return to the United States, he

00:11:31.986 --> 00:11:33.946
set forth to build a life, all the while

00:11:34.236 --> 00:11:36.396
keeping silent about what he had seen.

00:11:36.876 --> 00:11:39.636
He started a family, pursued a Ph.D.,

00:11:39.666 --> 00:11:42.068
built his career, and became involved

00:11:42.216 --> 00:11:43.596
with the civil rights movement.

00:11:44.406 --> 00:11:46.219
And years later, the assassination of

00:11:46.596 --> 00:11:48.755
Dr. Martin Luther King in 1968

00:11:49.566 --> 00:11:50.566
shook him to the core.

00:11:50.706 --> 00:11:52.565
It had a profound impact on him.

00:11:52.986 --> 00:11:54.848
And in his testimony, he describes the

00:11:55.116 --> 00:11:57.305
heartache and horror he felt

00:11:57.306 --> 00:11:58.776
in its wake, particularly when

00:11:59.526 --> 00:12:01.896
he confronted the pain and disillusionment

00:12:02.226 --> 00:12:03.845
of many of the young Black and brown

00:12:03.846 --> 00:12:05.953
students at the high school where he served

00:12:06.126 --> 00:12:07.245
as a principal.

00:12:07.896 --> 00:12:10.096
And one day, these present

00:12:10.363 --> 00:12:12.225
realities in the United States and his

00:12:12.673 --> 00:12:14.567
memories of the war collided

00:12:14.923 --> 00:12:16.540
when a visitor came to one of his

00:12:16.753 --> 00:12:17.753
classrooms.

00:12:18.973 --> 00:12:21.042
And one day I came to a classroom,

00:12:22.273 --> 00:12:23.953
and in that classroom there was a lady

00:12:24.883 --> 00:12:26.892
and she was trying to talk to the young men

00:12:26.893 --> 00:12:28.183
who were not listening to her,

00:12:29.353 --> 00:12:30.702
you know, they doing everything but

00:12:30.703 --> 00:12:31.703
listening.

00:12:32.113 --> 00:12:33.702
And I stood at the door and I began to

00:12:33.703 --> 00:12:35.442
realize that she was a survivor

00:12:36.283 --> 00:12:37.842
and she had survived one of the worst

00:12:37.843 --> 00:12:39.363
camps. She had survived Auschwitz.

00:12:40.093 --> 00:12:42.162
And she was here today, on this particular

00:12:42.163 --> 00:12:44.083
day to share her experience, her pain,

00:12:44.533 --> 00:12:45.533
if you will.

00:12:45.553 --> 00:12:48.073
And these young men were not listening.

00:12:48.463 --> 00:12:50.823
They had their own pain, you know, and

00:12:50.833 --> 00:12:52.242
I understood that, you know, the pain of

00:12:52.243 --> 00:12:54.703
rejection, the pain of not giving, being

00:12:54.973 --> 00:12:56.833
given the kind of recognition you deserve.

00:12:56.983 --> 00:12:58.002
I understood all of that.

00:12:58.003 --> 00:12:59.424
I had been through there, and

00:13:00.313 --> 00:13:01.979
I knew their pain, but I also knew

00:13:02.593 --> 00:13:03.593
her pain.

00:13:04.513 --> 00:13:06.222
And so I had to say to them, hold it,

00:13:06.223 --> 00:13:07.223
fellas, cool it.

00:13:07.753 --> 00:13:10.152
Listen, what she says

00:13:10.153 --> 00:13:11.563
is true. I was there.

00:13:12.703 --> 00:13:14.623
And so they got quiet for a while.

00:13:15.553 --> 00:13:17.623
And once she started telling

00:13:18.223 --> 00:13:20.833
her account of what happened at Auschwitz,

00:13:22.123 --> 00:13:23.123
they really listened.

00:13:23.683 --> 00:13:24.972
And she told them how she lost her

00:13:24.973 --> 00:13:26.173
grandmother. Grandfather.

00:13:28.033 --> 00:13:29.993
Her parents, brothers, sisters, cousins,

00:13:30.553 --> 00:13:31.992
all those near and dear who had come into

00:13:31.993 --> 00:13:34.272
that camp, all of them had

00:13:34.273 --> 00:13:36.135
gone to the gas chambers and had ended

00:13:36.523 --> 00:13:37.523
up in the ovens.

00:13:38.053 --> 00:13:40.423
And of her family, she was the only one

00:13:41.053 --> 00:13:42.053
that came out of there.

00:13:44.083 --> 00:13:45.083
And they listen, she

00:13:46.303 --> 00:13:47.303
shared with them so much.

00:13:49.003 --> 00:13:50.622
And they ask her questions, which made me

00:13:50.623 --> 00:13:52.303
know that she

00:13:53.373 --> 00:13:54.373
had to reach them.

00:13:55.313 --> 00:13:56.572
They came up and looked at the numbers

00:13:56.573 --> 00:13:57.772
tattooed on her arm and

00:13:58.943 --> 00:14:00.443
they thanked her

00:14:01.673 --> 00:14:03.682
and they did something that hadn't done in

00:14:03.683 --> 00:14:05.002
a long while. They left that room in

00:14:05.003 --> 00:14:06.003
silence.

00:14:07.133 --> 00:14:08.872
And, of course, she turned to me and of

00:14:08.873 --> 00:14:10.603
course, tears were coming down her face.

00:14:10.613 --> 00:14:12.810
And she thanked me for my intervention

00:14:12.893 --> 00:14:13.893
and

00:14:15.143 --> 00:14:16.515
wanted to know more about my

00:14:17.423 --> 00:14:18.623
experience at Buchenwald.

00:14:20.693 --> 00:14:22.433
And I began to remember.

00:14:23.273 --> 00:14:25.313
Bass's experience was unique,

00:14:25.793 --> 00:14:27.704
but what he made of that experience and

00:14:27.983 --> 00:14:30.412
how he and his words began to hook

00:14:30.413 --> 00:14:33.022
things together in subsequent decades

00:14:33.353 --> 00:14:34.943
is instructive for us all.

00:14:35.693 --> 00:14:37.732
Bass's memories of military service and

00:14:37.733 --> 00:14:40.042
camp liberation, and how those memories

00:14:40.043 --> 00:14:41.856
came to bear on his interpretation of

00:14:42.173 --> 00:14:43.673
racism in the United States.

00:14:44.183 --> 00:14:45.823
It results in a call for unity.

00:14:46.343 --> 00:14:48.401
His testimony concludes with a call for an

00:14:48.593 --> 00:14:50.962
antiracist coalition building

00:14:50.963 --> 00:14:51.963
force.

00:14:52.013 --> 00:14:53.962
But he also closes with a call for

00:14:53.963 --> 00:14:54.963
vigilance.

00:14:55.553 --> 00:14:57.268
The Holocaust forced a global moral

00:14:57.743 --> 00:14:58.743
reckoning.

00:14:58.763 --> 00:15:00.352
In the United States, it was a tipping

00:15:00.353 --> 00:15:02.723
point that compelled many white Americans

00:15:02.993 --> 00:15:05.303
to examine the state of their own country

00:15:05.633 --> 00:15:08.122
and to get behind African-Americans demands

00:15:08.273 --> 00:15:10.673
for fundamental civil and human rights.

00:15:11.523 --> 00:15:13.434
But the realities of the Holocaust also

00:15:13.973 --> 00:15:16.132
played a significant part in forcing white

00:15:16.133 --> 00:15:17.456
supremacists to abandon the

00:15:18.353 --> 00:15:19.725
most explicit and chauvinist

00:15:20.573 --> 00:15:22.239
articulations of their worldviews,

00:15:23.033 --> 00:15:24.832
right, lest they be associated with the

00:15:24.833 --> 00:15:26.058
unsightly backwardness of

00:15:27.083 --> 00:15:28.083
the recent war.

00:15:28.703 --> 00:15:30.721
And it's significant that Bass reengaged

00:15:31.643 --> 00:15:33.322
with his memories of Buchenwald around

00:15:33.323 --> 00:15:34.452
1970.

00:15:34.943 --> 00:15:37.042
Formal narratives and institutions of

00:15:37.043 --> 00:15:38.752
Holocaust memory were on the rise in the

00:15:38.753 --> 00:15:40.363
United States around that time.

00:15:41.003 --> 00:15:42.669
But as he so delicately puts it in

00:15:43.193 --> 00:15:45.352
his testimony, this was a moment

00:15:45.353 --> 00:15:47.632
when many of the promises of civil rights

00:15:47.633 --> 00:15:49.013
had begun to falter.

00:15:49.643 --> 00:15:51.260
And the government and many white

00:15:51.803 --> 00:15:54.383
Americans were clearly coming up short

00:15:54.683 --> 00:15:56.055
in the project of reflection

00:15:56.933 --> 00:15:59.722
and reckoning, the project of dismantling

00:15:59.723 --> 00:16:02.332
and rebuilding their racist foundations.

00:16:02.753 --> 00:16:04.183
And Bass saw that failure.

00:16:04.193 --> 00:16:06.173
He saw it in the refusal,

00:16:06.773 --> 00:16:08.962
you know, of his community to change, but

00:16:08.963 --> 00:16:10.791
most especially among his young students.

00:16:11.933 --> 00:16:13.762
Really, Bass's memories open up the

00:16:13.763 --> 00:16:16.282
possibility of productive comparison

00:16:16.283 --> 00:16:18.733
toward solidarity and racial justice.

00:16:19.553 --> 00:16:21.121
He asked us to consider not only

00:16:21.743 --> 00:16:23.902
what changed after 1945, but

00:16:23.903 --> 00:16:26.010
also what attitudes and cultures stayed the

00:16:26.093 --> 00:16:28.192
same and,

00:16:28.583 --> 00:16:30.802
in particular, stayed the same, cloaked

00:16:30.803 --> 00:16:32.175
or carried on in innuendo or

00:16:32.963 --> 00:16:33.963
in action.

00:16:34.553 --> 00:16:36.742
And as an educator, Bass concludes his

00:16:36.743 --> 00:16:39.292
testimony with an important lesson

00:16:39.293 --> 00:16:40.293
for us all.

00:16:41.403 --> 00:16:42.602
And look what it did to us.

00:16:43.473 --> 00:16:45.542
Racism has just about split us so many

00:16:45.543 --> 00:16:47.162
different ways that we can't live in

00:16:47.163 --> 00:16:49.623
harmony with one another, suspicious.

00:16:50.263 --> 00:16:51.933
All of this I discovered

00:16:54.093 --> 00:16:56.282
as an adult and I didn't get it when I

00:16:56.283 --> 00:16:57.602
was young, when I should have had it

00:16:58.173 --> 00:16:59.582
because people didn't want to deal with it.

00:16:59.583 --> 00:17:01.690
It was an ugly side of our history and ugly

00:17:01.713 --> 00:17:03.012
things we want to push under the rug.

00:17:03.033 --> 00:17:04.033
We don't want to deal with it.

00:17:04.123 --> 00:17:06.812
And I'm saying to people today, you must

00:17:07.293 --> 00:17:08.293
face it.

00:17:08.582 --> 00:17:10.863
You can't laundry and sanitize our history.

00:17:10.953 --> 00:17:12.962
You must take history with its beauty and

00:17:13.113 --> 00:17:14.762
you must take it with its degradation.

00:17:14.763 --> 00:17:16.952
You've got to deal with it, for us to

00:17:16.953 --> 00:17:18.864
be whole human beings and and to make a

00:17:19.082 --> 00:17:20.612
difference. And it's especially true with

00:17:20.613 --> 00:17:21.613
young people.

00:17:21.783 --> 00:17:24.032
And so I go around telling my

00:17:24.033 --> 00:17:25.561
story. And, of course, I, you know, I get

00:17:25.562 --> 00:17:26.983
the people saying, Leon, what

00:17:27.723 --> 00:17:29.612
do you deal with this? This is, this is not

00:17:29.613 --> 00:17:30.613
a Black problem.

00:17:30.753 --> 00:17:32.792
I said, hey, it's not a Black problem.

00:17:32.793 --> 00:17:33.793
It's not a white problem.

00:17:33.903 --> 00:17:34.903
It's a human problem.

00:17:36.063 --> 00:17:37.453
And we've got to face it.

00:17:38.433 --> 00:17:39.952
And as Dr. King said, injustice

00:17:41.283 --> 00:17:43.502
anywhere is the loss

00:17:43.503 --> 00:17:44.552
of justice everywhere.

00:17:44.553 --> 00:17:46.443
You see words to that effect

00:17:46.723 --> 00:17:48.192
and it's true. What affects you, affects

00:17:48.543 --> 00:17:50.792
me. Your pain has to be my pain and my

00:17:50.793 --> 00:17:51.843
pain has to be your pain.

00:17:52.083 --> 00:17:54.190
And I must somehow convey that to the young

00:17:54.213 --> 00:17:56.102
people that I come to.

00:17:56.433 --> 00:17:57.952
It's my commitment and somebody

00:17:58.953 --> 00:18:00.423
says you, why do you deal with

00:18:01.113 --> 00:18:03.212
that mess? You know, I said, I'm sorry.

00:18:03.603 --> 00:18:05.193
I know it's been 40 something years,

00:18:05.823 --> 00:18:07.443
but that doesn't make it go away.

00:18:08.613 --> 00:18:10.653
It only makes us become more aware

00:18:10.803 --> 00:18:12.663
that we today have to do something that

00:18:13.083 --> 00:18:15.512
to stop that which created

00:18:16.263 --> 00:18:17.341
the Final Solution and

00:18:18.423 --> 00:18:20.673
that something is racism,

00:18:21.753 --> 00:18:23.272
really racism is at the root of

00:18:23.913 --> 00:18:25.652
all of this. Under that umbrella comes

00:18:25.653 --> 00:18:27.812
bigotry and prejudice and discrimination,

00:18:27.813 --> 00:18:30.512
unemployment, people who are unemployable,

00:18:30.873 --> 00:18:32.702
large institutions filled with those who

00:18:32.703 --> 00:18:33.933
are drug addicts and those who are

00:18:34.083 --> 00:18:35.896
criminals, or because somehow we have

00:18:36.423 --> 00:18:38.150
come to grips with that institution called

00:18:38.313 --> 00:18:40.532
racism, and we have

00:18:40.533 --> 00:18:42.572
to because we see the ultimate of racism,

00:18:42.573 --> 00:18:43.982
which was what I saw at Buchenwald.

00:18:45.123 --> 00:18:46.123
And so what do we do?

00:18:46.353 --> 00:18:48.241
Do we as educators, and

00:18:49.473 --> 00:18:51.032
I use it in the broadest sense because we

00:18:51.033 --> 00:18:52.797
are all teachers of the young and in

00:18:53.403 --> 00:18:55.773
our way of life,

00:18:56.103 --> 00:18:57.723
do we convey to them

00:18:58.263 --> 00:19:00.363
the best that we can be,

00:19:00.483 --> 00:19:01.855
or do we give them the worst

00:19:02.763 --> 00:19:03.763
side that we have?

00:19:04.533 --> 00:19:06.572
Do we promote racism

00:19:06.813 --> 00:19:08.373
through our apathy?

00:19:09.123 --> 00:19:11.643
When we hear and see things, we say nothing

00:19:12.813 --> 00:19:14.492
because we don't want to jeopardize that

00:19:14.493 --> 00:19:15.572
which is important to us.

00:19:15.603 --> 00:19:17.514
Our investment in a job, our investment

00:19:18.123 --> 00:19:19.123
among friends.

00:19:19.413 --> 00:19:20.913
We don't want to disturb our families.

00:19:20.923 --> 00:19:23.029
So no matter what people say

00:19:23.223 --> 00:19:25.472
or do, just leave it alone, sweep

00:19:25.473 --> 00:19:27.633
it under the rug and somehow it'll go away.

00:19:28.323 --> 00:19:29.672
But the skinheads are with us.

00:19:29.943 --> 00:19:30.943
The Klan is with us.

00:19:31.023 --> 00:19:32.642
It may be dressed in Brooks Brothers suit

00:19:32.643 --> 00:19:33.693
with attaché cases.

00:19:33.813 --> 00:19:36.062
They may be in, in, doctors,

00:19:36.063 --> 00:19:38.023
they may be lawyers. They may be the guy

00:19:38.163 --> 00:19:40.202
who drives the bus, but they are still with

00:19:40.203 --> 00:19:40.443
us.

